<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  =============================================================================
  Maven module descriptor: card-service (the card bounded context)
  =============================================================================

  Purpose:
    Builds the deployable service that owns everything about a credit card in
    the migrated CardDemo system: listing cards with paging, reading one card,
    and updating one card. It owns exactly one PostgreSQL schema, "card", which
    holds a single table, "card.cards", and no other module writes to it. All
    Java in this module lives under the package root com.carddemo.card.

  Parameters (inherited configuration):
    parent            com.carddemo:carddemo-services:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT
                      (the reactor aggregator at services/pom.xml)
    groupId           inherited from that parent; deliberately not restated
    version           inherited from that parent; deliberately not restated
    java release      inherited from the parent toolchain configuration
    dependency
    versions          inherited, every one of them, from the parent's
                      dependencyManagement and the Spring Boot BOM it imports.
                      This file contains no version element on any dependency.
    Checkstyle
    binding           inherited from the parent, which binds the documentation
                      gate to the validate phase for all modules at once.

  Return values (build output):
    One executable Spring Boot layered jar at the deterministic path
    target/card-service.jar. The path is deterministic because <finalName> is
    pinned below; services/card-service/Dockerfile copies that exact path, so
    the name is a contract between these two files rather than a preference.

  Exceptions or errors (build failure modes a reader will meet):
    1. Checkstyle runs in the validate phase with failOnViolation set true and
       violationSeverity set to warning. A missing Javadoc comment, or a source
       package with no package-info.java, therefore fails "mvn validate" before
       the compiler is ever invoked. That ordering is intentional: the
       documentation gate is cheap and should reject early.
    2. The shared Checkstyle configuration declares its SuppressionFilter fail
       closed (optional set to false). config/checkstyle/suppressions.xml must
       be reachable from the build's working tree or validate aborts outright
       rather than silently continuing with no suppressions in force.
    3. spring-boot-starter-flyway without flyway-database-postgresql does not
       fail the build. It fails at application
       startup instead. See the note on flyway-database-postgresql below.
    4. CardRepositoryIT is to run under Failsafe against a real database started
       by Testcontainers, so a host with no working container runtime will fail
       the integration phase even when every unit test passes. That class is
       authored at a later index of the same plan and does not exist yet; the
       Failsafe binding below is what will make it run when it lands.

  Provenance (all paths below are REFERENCE ONLY and are never modified):
    app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl   1459 lines  card list, the canonical CICS browse
    app/cbl/COCRDSLC.cbl    887 lines  card detail read
    app/cbl/COCRDUPC.cbl   1560 lines  card update
    app/cbl/CBACT02C.cbl    178 lines  sequential card file reader, read path
    app/cpy/CVACT02Y.cpy     14 lines  01 CARD-RECORD, record length 150
    The copybook is the storage contract this module's schema is derived from:
    CARD-NUM X(16), CARD-ACCT-ID 9(11), CARD-CVV-CD 9(03), CARD-EMBOSSED-NAME
    X(50), CARD-EXPIRAION-DATE X(10) (misspelled in the baseline and corrected
    to expiration_date in the target), CARD-ACTIVE-STATUS X(01), then FILLER
    X(59) which is padding to the fixed record length and is dropped. Those
    widths sum to 150, which is why the record length is what it is.

  Assumptions:
    XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids a doubled hyphen anywhere inside a comment, and
    a violation is a hard parse failure rather than a warning. Every horizontal
    ruler in this file is therefore built from "=" or "~" and never from the
    hyphen, because a run of hyphens contains the forbidden pair by
    construction. For the same reason prose ranges here are written with the
    word "to" instead of a dash. A maintainer reaching for a hyphen ruler will
    break this build, so the constraint is recorded rather than left to be
    rediscovered.

  Documentation standard:
    Governing rule:   user-specified Rule 1 (Explainability)
    Prose convention: docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md
    Mechanical gate:  config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml
    Alternatives Considered: an earlier revision of this header made the linter
    configuration authoritative wherever it and the prose disagreed, on the
    ground that the linter is what decides whether the build passes. That is
    rejected. The rule and the prose standard are AUTHORITATIVE, and the gate
    implements a subset of them: it checks Javadoc presence and completeness and
    cannot read a rationale at all, so it passes code the rule fails. Treating
    the gate as final would let one attribute value silently narrow the
    obligation, which is how an undocumented member ships behind a green build.
    Where the two disagree, the gate is what gets corrected.
  =============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    Assumptions: the parent is the reactor aggregator at services/pom.xml, and
    the explicit relativePath is what lets this module be read from a clean
    checkout in which the parent has never been installed into a local
    repository. Without it Maven would look for the parent in the repository
    first, so a container build running
    "mvn -f services/pom.xml -pl card-service -am package" against a fresh
    ~/.m2 would fail to resolve its own parent.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <!--
    Assumptions: groupId and version are intentionally absent here, not
    forgotten. Maven inherits both from the parent above, and restating them
    would create a second place to edit at the next version bump plus a silent
    drift path if only one of the two is updated. A reader tempted to "fix" the
    omission should leave it alone.
  -->
  <artifactId>card-service</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>CardDemo Card Service</name>
  <description>
    Card bounded context for the migrated CardDemo system, covering card list
    (COCRDLIC), card detail (COCRDSLC), card update (COCRDUPC) and the
    sequential card read path (CBACT02C).
  </description>

  <!--
    Alternatives Considered: a module local <properties> block restating the
    Java release level, the source encoding or any dependency version. Rejected
    and therefore absent entirely: every one of those values is already managed
    by the parent, so duplicating one here would give the project two places to
    change and would let this module drift from its eight siblings the moment
    only one of the two copies is updated. Nothing in this module needs a value
    the parent does not already supply, so the correct block is no block.
  -->

  <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Dependencies
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Governing principle: every version used below is inherited from the parent's
    dependencyManagement and from the Spring Boot BOM that parent imports. No
    version element appears on any dependency in this file.

    Trade-offs: the accepted cost of that rule is real, and it is worth naming
    because it looks like an omission. Reading this file alone does not tell you
    which version of anything you will get, so answering that question means
    opening the parent as well. The alternative, pinning each version at the point
    of use where it would be visible, was rejected because nine modules pinning
    the same artifact independently is nine places to update and eight chances to
    miss one, and a divergence between two services on, for example, the Flyway
    version would surface as a runtime migration difference rather than as a build
    error.

    Assumptions: the set is closed rather than open. Each entry traces to one of
    the four migrated programs or to the card record contract described in the
    header, so an addition that cannot be traced that way belongs in a different
    context, not in this one.
  -->
  <dependencies>

    <!--
      Assumptions: common-lib is the shared kernel and the only sibling this
      module compiles against. It supplies Money (BigDecimal held at scale 2
      with HALF_UP rounding), PageResponse (the keyset page envelope), ApiError
      with GlobalExceptionHandler, JwtRoleConverter, FieldValidationFlag and the
      fixed-width and zoned-decimal codecs, all under com.carddemo.common. Those
      types are consumed rather than redeclared per service so that a card
      payload and an account payload cannot disagree about what a money value or
      a page cursor is.

      Trade-offs: ${project.version} is used in place of a literal version so
      the sibling always moves with the reactor. The accepted cost is that this
      module cannot be built against an older common-lib without editing the
      POM, which is the correct bias here because the two are released together
      from one reactor.

      This is the only sibling dependency this module may declare. No other
      service module belongs here. The layering test forbids importing another
      service's domain package, and keeping that coupling out of the build graph
      is what prevents the rule being satisfied on paper while a transitive path
      quietly reintroduces the dependency.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: these three starters carry the three categories of CICS verb
      this context actually used. SEND MAP and RECEIVE MAP become JSON responses
      and requests (web); the keyed reads and the browse at
      app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl:1129 through :1376 become repository calls (data-jpa);
      and the field-level validation chain in app/cbl/COCRDUPC.cbl becomes
      declarative constraints (validation). The constraint widths are not free
      choices either: each is the picture width from app/cpy/CVACT02Y.cpy, so the
      fixed field length the 3270 screen enforced still holds at the API edge.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the baseline CICS transaction definitions for all
      three card transactions declare RESSEC(NO) and CMDSEC(NO): see
      app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD:354 for CCDL (card detail, COCRDSLC), :364 for CCLI
      (card list, COCRDLIC) and :375 for CCUP (card update, COCRDUPC). CICS
      therefore performed no resource-level or command-level authorization for
      them, and the only admin versus user distinction available to the programs
      was the CDEMO-USER-TYPE field travelling in the COMMAREA that the terminal
      echoed back between turns. A resource server validating a signed token
      moves that decision onto the server, where a client cannot assert its own
      role at all.

      This is a difference in what the two platforms provide rather than a defect
      in the COBOL: on the mainframe an external security manager covered this
      ground outside the application, and that component has no in-process
      equivalent on this side, so its job is redistributed onto token validation
      here and onto task role policy in the infrastructure tree.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: two consumers outside this module poll the health endpoint
      this starter publishes. The load balancer target group uses it to decide
      whether a task receives traffic, and the HEALTHCHECK instruction in
      services/card-service/Dockerfile uses it to decide whether the container is
      up. Removing this dependency breaks both, and it breaks them at deploy time
      rather than at build time, so the coupling is written down here.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: runtime scope rather than compile scope because no source file
      in this module names a driver class. The driver is located through the
      java.sql.Driver service loader from the JDBC URL alone. Keeping it off the
      compile classpath means application code cannot accidentally import a
      PostgreSQL-specific type and bind the card context to one engine at the
      source level, which is the coupling the repository interface exists to
      avoid.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this module carries the one migration that creates the schema
      it owns, src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__card.sql, so schema ownership
      and code ownership live in the same deployable and cannot be applied out of
      step with each other.

      Assumptions: the first artifact below is the STARTER, and naming it rather
      than flyway-core is what actually activates migration. Spring Boot 4 split
      its autoconfiguration into one module per technology, and
      FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in spring-boot-flyway, which only this
      starter puts on the classpath. Declaring flyway-core alone resolves,
      compiles and starts, and then applies no migration whatsoever: the
      spring.flyway.* keys in application.yml bind to nothing, the schema history
      table is never created, and the first card query fails on a missing
      relation.

      Refactoring Rationale: flyway-core is no longer declared in its own right.
      The starter brings it transitively at the version the aggregator's
      flyway.version property pins, so a direct declaration would add a second
      place that version could be edited while adding nothing to the classpath.

      The second artifact below is a mandatory companion, not a redundant copy of
      the first. Flyway 10 moved database-specific support out of flyway-core into
      one module per database, and the starter pulls no database module. Without
      it the build compiles perfectly and then fails at application startup, when
      Flyway cannot find an implementation matching a postgresql JDBC URL. There
      is no compile-time signal for the omission whatsoever, which is precisely
      why it is called out. It is not a duplicate and it must not be removed as
      one.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this serves the OpenAPI 3.1 document described by
      src/main/resources/openapi/card-api.yaml, and the browser client for the
      card screens is written against that document. The contract is the
      integration point between two separately built trees, so the publisher
      belongs in the runtime dependency set. A build-time-only generator was the
      alternative and would emit a file that nothing serves, leaving no way for a
      client to discover a drifted contract at run time.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: runtime scope because no source file imports the registry
      type; actuator autoconfiguration discovers it on the classpath. Compile
      scope would advertise an API this module deliberately does not call, since
      the common metric tags are configured once in common-lib rather than
      per service.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Test scope
      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Assumptions: the five test artifacts below are declared here rather than
      left to inheritance. The parent aggregator's contract is dependencyManagement
      only, meaning it fixes the version of each artifact without contributing any
      dependency of its own, so there is nothing on the test classpath to inherit
      in the first place. Declaring them is also the safe direction of error should
      that contract ever widen: a child entry carrying no version merges with an
      identical parent entry on the same groupId and artifactId and resolves to
      exactly the same artifact, so no drift is possible; whereas omitting an entry
      the parent does not supply leaves this module's test tree uncompilable, which
      is the more expensive mistake of the two.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the controller test is a @WebMvcTest against a controller
      sitting behind the resource server filter chain. This artifact supplies the
      JWT request post processor used to present a token carrying the
      carddemo-admin group, which is how the admin-only card detail route is
      exercised and how the non-admin rejection is asserted. It is not bundled
      inside spring-boot-starter-test, so without it neither assertion can be
      written at all.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: these are the Testcontainers 2.x coordinates and the
      artifactId prefix is load-bearing. Spring Boot imports testcontainers-bom,
      and version 2 of that BOM renamed every module away from its 1.x form: it
      manages testcontainers-postgresql and testcontainers-junit-jupiter and
      contains no entry named postgresql or junit-jupiter at all. Using the 1.x
      names here yields "dependencies.dependency.version for
      org.testcontainers:postgresql:jar is missing" and the POM cannot even be
      read, because there is no managed version to inherit. Both versions come
      from that BOM through the parent and must never be restated here.

      Alternatives Considered: an embedded in-memory database for the repository
      integration test. Rejected because that test exists to prove the by-account
      query which replaces the CARDAIX alternate index
      (app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD:13 defines that file, app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl:217 names
      it) behaves correctly, and all three properties it checks are engine
      behaviours: ordering under a keyset predicate, CHAR(16) blank padding on the
      card number, and whether the planner actually selects the secondary index.
      An in-memory engine reproduces none of the three faithfully, so a green test
      against one would say nothing about the deployed database.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the layering rules are authored once in common-lib's test
      tree, but an ArchUnit rule can only analyse classes present on the
      classpath of the module whose tests execute it. Declaring the artifact here
      is what allows this module's own tests to apply those rules to
      com.carddemo.card, in particular the prohibition on float and double
      anywhere in the money path and the prohibition on importing another
      service's domain package. Leaving it out would make the rules authored but
      unenforced for this module, which is not distinguishable from not having
      written them.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the engine above is necessary and was not
      sufficient, and the comment beside it states exactly why: a rule can only
      analyse the classpath of the module whose tests execute it. The rule CLASS
      still had to arrive here, and it did not. Maven resolution passes a
      module's main classes to its consumers and never its test classes, so
      com.carddemo.card was never analysed by anything; the engine sat here with
      no rules to run. This artifact is the shared kernel's test jar, narrowed to
      the architecture package, and the architecture-rules Surefire execution in
      services/pom.xml is what scans it into this module's test run.

      Assumptions: the rules remain authored exactly once, at
      services/common-lib/src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/LayeringRulesTest.java.
      This declaration delivers that one class and adds no copy of it. Version
      and scope come from the aggregator's managed set, so neither is written
      here and neither can drift away from the other seven services.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <type>test-jar</type>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

  </dependencies>

  <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Deliberate non-adoptions
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Alternatives Considered: the following are each a reasonable thing to expect
    in a Spring Boot service POM, and each is absent on purpose. They are listed
    with their specific reason so that a reader does not read absence as an
    oversight and add one back.

    Lombok. The inherited Checkstyle configuration requires a Javadoc comment on
    methods and does not exempt property accessors, so accessors and constructors
    generated at compile time carry no documentation the linter can see. Adopting
    Lombok would leave two options, both unacceptable: suppress the whole of
    src/main/java from the documentation gate, or ship a module that cannot pass
    its own validate phase. Java 21 record types with explicit constructors give
    the same brevity while leaving every member somewhere a Javadoc comment can
    be attached.

    MapStruct. Mapping the card record onto DTOs in this module is not a
    mechanical field copy, so a generated mapper has nowhere to put the reasoning
    each step needs. Four of the steps are judgements: the 59-byte FILLER at
    app/cpy/CVACT02Y.cpy:11 is dropped because it is padding to the fixed record
    length and not data; the primary account number is masked to its last four
    digits on every response except the administrative detail route; the card
    verification value is never serialised at all; and the baseline's misspelled
    CARD-EXPIRAION-DATE at app/cpy/CVACT02Y.cpy:9 is renamed to expirationDate,
    which is a rename that has to be traceable because the baseline field keeps
    its original spelling forever. Hand-written mappers hold that reasoning at
    the point of use.

    resilience4j and spring-retry. Spring Framework 7 arrives through the Spring
    Boot parent and provides retry in the framework core, so a library would add
    a second, competing retry vocabulary for a capability already present. The
    core annotation is Retryable with a maxRetries attribute, where total
    attempts are one plus that value, and it is switched on with
    EnableResilientMethods rather than the older EnableRetry that predates the
    move into core.

    A circuit breaker. This module makes no synchronous outbound call to another
    service, so there is no dependency for a breaker to protect. Adding one would
    introduce a new failure mode of its own, an open circuit rejecting requests
    the database would have served, while removing none.

    Redis or ElastiCache. The baseline reads its card file directly with no cache
    tier anywhere in front of it. Introducing one here would add an invalidation
    contract that no golden master expresses an expectation about, so a stale read
    would be a behaviour change that the parity comparison could not detect.

    Kafka or Kinesis. Out of scope for the project, and unnecessary here in any
    case: this module neither publishes nor consumes a message. The baseline's
    decoupled messaging extensions belong to other contexts, not to the card
    screens.

    spring-boot-starter-batch. None of the four migrated programs is a nightly
    job. CBACT02C is a read-and-print utility, and its logic becomes the read
    path on the card repository rather than a chunk-oriented batch step, so a job
    repository and its schema would be provisioned for nothing.

    An SQS client, spring-cloud-aws, or an AWS SDK messaging module. This module
    defines no queue configuration and no message listener, so a messaging client
    would enlarge both the image and the task role's required permissions with no
    caller.

    A read replica or multi-datasource routing library. Read replicas are out of
    scope for the project; reporting reads are served from read-only cross-schema
    views on the writer, so this module has exactly one datasource and needs no
    routing decision at all.
  -->

  <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Build
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Governing principle: the parent owns the plugin set. This section declares
    only what is genuinely specific to this module and restates no version.

    Trade-offs: the Checkstyle documentation gate is inherited deliberately and is
    not re-declared here. Re-declaring it would open three ways to get it wrong at
    once: re-pinning the Checkstyle version so this module lints under a different
    ruleset than its eight siblings, duplicating the configLocation so the two
    copies can diverge, or relaxing the setting that makes a violation fail the
    build. Single-sourcing it in the parent is what keeps every module gated
    identically. Accepted cost: reading this file alone does not tell you which
    rules apply, so the pointer in the header block above exists to close that
    gap.

    For the avoidance of doubt, this module must never weaken that gate: not by
    passing the Checkstyle skip property on a command line, not by adding a
    plugin-level skip flag, not by turning off the setting that makes a violation
    fail the build, and not by tolerating a non-zero return code in any pipeline
    that builds it. No suppressionsLocation is declared here and there is no
    module-local Checkstyle or suppressions file; both live only under
    config/checkstyle so that all modules share one ruleset.

    Assumptions: Surefire is deliberately not declared, because the default
    lifecycle for jar packaging already binds it. Class names ending in Test are
    picked up by its default include pattern and run in the test phase with no
    help from this file.

    Failsafe is the opposite case and is therefore declared below. The Spring Boot
    parent carries maven-failsafe-plugin in pluginManagement only, which fixes its
    version and pre-binds its goals but never adds it to the build itself, so a
    module that stays silent about it gets no integration test phase at all. The
    include pattern was never the missing piece; the binding was. This was
    verified by running the reactor both ways against a probe class: with no
    entry, an IT-suffixed class is never executed, no failsafe report directory is
    produced, and the build still reports success, which is worse than having no
    integration test because it looks like coverage that does not exist. That is
    exactly the failure this entry exists to prevent for CardRepositoryIT, which
    is authored at a later index of the same plan.
  -->
  <build>
    <!--
      Assumptions: this pins the build output to target/card-service.jar instead
      of target/card-service-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar, and
      services/card-service/Dockerfile copies that exact literal path. A version-suffixed
      name would force the Dockerfile to match the jar with a wildcard,
      which keeps working right up until target holds two jars, for instance
      after a version change with no intervening clean, and then copies the wrong
      one with no error. This name is a contract between this file and that
      Dockerfile, so neither may change it alone.
    -->
    <finalName>card-service</finalName>

    <plugins>
      <!--
        Assumptions: this plugin contributes the repackage goal, which rewrites
        the plain jar produced by the jar plugin into the executable layered jar
        the Dockerfile's runtime stage expects. Without it the runtime stage would
        receive an archive with no Main-Class entry in its manifest and no nested
        dependency layout, and the container would exit immediately instead of
        starting the service. The version is supplied by the Spring Boot parent on
        purpose: pinning it here would let the plugin drift away from the Boot
        version whose jar layout it is written to produce.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Assumptions: this entry is what makes the module's integration test run at
        all. The Spring Boot parent declares maven-failsafe-plugin in
        pluginManagement, which supplies its version and pre-binds its
        integration-test and verify goals, but it does not add the plugin to the
        build, so inheritance alone leaves it inert. Listing it here with no
        version and no configuration is the minimum that activates it: the version
        and both goals still come from the parent, and the default include pattern
        for the integration test suffix is already exactly what CardRepositoryIT
        will need, so no includes block is added and none should be.

        Trade-offs: this repeats a plugin coordinate that a reader might expect to
        be purely inherited, and the parent could later bind the plugin itself,
        which would make this entry redundant. That redundancy is accepted because
        it is harmless (Maven merges the two declarations on the same coordinate
        and the goals carry the same identity) whereas the failure it prevents is
        silent: without it the integration test phase does not happen and the
        build still reports success, so the gap would show up as absent coverage
        rather than as a red build. This is the same mechanism that makes the
        spring-boot-maven-plugin entry above necessary rather than optional.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
